## Migration Analysis

Your code has these patterns:
1. **Cancellable work loop** — `while (_running)` loop
2. **ThreadAbortException as control flow** — catch block rolls back transactions
3. **Thread.ResetAbort** — continues loop after abort
4. **Blocking call interruption** — `GetNextItem()` may block

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — mirrors the old ThreadAbortException handler
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed; loop condition handles continuation vs exit
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes

| Old (.NET Framework) | New (.NET 8) |
|---------------------|--------------|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — loop naturally continues or exits |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` then abort | `Thread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2))` |

## Critical: Unblock `GetNextItem`

The old `Thread.Abort` forcibly interrupted blocking calls. Cooperative cancellation **cannot** do this unless the blocking code checks the token. You must modify `GetNextItem`:

```csharp
// If using a BlockingCollection<T>:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}

// If using a custom wait:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!_itemAvailable.WaitOne(500))
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    }
    return DequeueItem();
}
```

## Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| Not passing token to `GetNextItem` | Thread stays blocked forever — `Stop()` hangs |
| Swallowing `OperationCanceledException` broadly | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to avoid catching unrelated cancellations |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks timer/callback resources |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | It only fires at the next checkpoint — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running |
| Replacing `Thread.Sleep(2000)` with another sleep | Use `Join(timeout)` — it actually waits for the thread to exit |

## If `GetNextItem` Cannot Be Modified

If the blocking call is in unmodifiable code (third-party library, native call), move it to a child process and use `Process.Kill()` as the last resort — this is the only way to forcibly stop uncooperative code in modern .NET.
